---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'Contact overtimechecker.com for corrections to overtime law citations, calculator errors, or guide feedback.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> with questions, corrections, or suggestions. We read every message and respond to substantive content questions within a few business days.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections to overtime law citations or calculation logic.</strong> Federal and state wage-and-hour law changes through regulatory updates, court decisions, and new Department of Labor guidance. If an overtime formula is documented incorrectly, a salary threshold has been updated, or a state-specific exemption is outdated, let us know with the specific page and the correction and we'll update promptly.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> Overtime law is complex, with significant variation between federal FLSA requirements and state-specific rules. If a guide contains an inaccuracy, omits an important caveat, or uses language that could mislead readers about their rights, we want to hear about it.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new content.</strong> If there's a state, exemption category, or overtime scenario we haven't covered that would be useful, we welcome requests. We track them and prioritize based on reader demand.</li>
    <li><strong>Technical issues.</strong> If the overtime calculator fails to load or produces an unexpected result, please include your browser, device, and the inputs you were using when you report the issue.</li>
    <li><strong>Press and partnership inquiries.</strong> Media inquiries about overtime policy, wage-and-hour trends, or calculator methodology are welcome.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't evaluate your specific wage dispute, advise on whether you have a viable overtime claim, or recommend specific attorneys or law firms. Our content describes the law in the general case — your specific employer, classification, and state may produce different results. For your specific situation, contact your state labor agency or a licensed wage-and-hour attorney in your state; many offer free or low-cost initial consultations. We also don't respond to partnership solicitations or paid content placement requests.</p>
</BaseLayout>
